                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "28c41897-0e95-32d0-a307-1d88635bfa60",
                        "entry_id": "fa76275e-d99e-37b6-b229-90dd35f91d0c",
                        "glosses": [
                            "To make glamorous."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "75f0f46f-f4d8-4811-bb55-1b1c5662141a",
                                "sense_id": "28c41897-0e95-32d0-a307-1d88635bfa60",
                                "text": "In her former blindness she had known him as shiftless Johnny Taylor, tall and lean. That was before the golden dust of pollen had beglamored his rags and her eyes."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "d56173de-43f4-48d1-b651-20884fb6d9fc",
                                "sense_id": "28c41897-0e95-32d0-a307-1d88635bfa60",
                                "text": "Lights glitter on wet planks, on the functional bodies of the day's catch, and floodlights beglamour the colored dresses and the pastel beach costumes of the crowds that stroll there."
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "85cdc93d-4e7e-3e57-a7e1-ffbf4989d8ae",
                        "entry_id": "fa76275e-d99e-37b6-b229-90dd35f91d0c",
                        "glosses": [
                            "To bedazzle; to deceive as if by magic."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "a5ce865e-05df-439a-8130-205285c51d83",
                                "sense_id": "85cdc93d-4e7e-3e57-a7e1-ffbf4989d8ae",
                                "text": "“Do,” cried Robin; “sir, she’s an auld withered hag, would spean a foal. Surely she did na sae beglamour your senses as to appear like a winsome young lass?”"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "906d3fbf-0037-48dc-9e66-d8d408b112bb",
                                "sense_id": "85cdc93d-4e7e-3e57-a7e1-ffbf4989d8ae",
                                "text": "It is not necessary to follow the wild goose chase which the Rev. George’s imagination ran from this starting-point to the moment when he was suddenly awakened, by an unmistakable symptom, to the fact that he was being outwitted and beglamoured, like the utter novice he was, by a power which he believed to be the devil."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "3066ad05-3206-4778-b250-9a69501210f6",
                                "sense_id": "85cdc93d-4e7e-3e57-a7e1-ffbf4989d8ae",
                                "text": "Imagination entered her second-hand from Nick, who himself had it third-hand, because Romance meanders from peddler to peddler, holding itself out for what it is not and defrauding the be-glamoured and credulous world."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "e23c4fe1-2320-4a54-93fa-a4c43d087ce5",
                                "sense_id": "85cdc93d-4e7e-3e57-a7e1-ffbf4989d8ae",
                                "text": "1972, Martha Rofheart, Cry ‘God for Harry’ (published in the U.S. as Fortune Made His Sword), London: Book Club Associates, 1972, Book 2, Chapter 7, p. 156,\nIt was easy to see how he had beglamored the youth of a nation."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
